7. Security Requirements
The ACL design must follow the principle of least privilege.

A user should receive only the permissions necessary for the operation they are authorized to perform.

The intended permission model is:

                    Institution Details
                           |
             +-------------+-------------+
             |             |             |
            READ          CREATE        WRITE
             |             |             |
            bb1           bb2           bb3
             |
       Branch condition
             |
            EEE

Delete permission is additionally controlled by:

DELETE → bb4

Therefore, the user's effective access depends on both:

User role

ACL operation

For the read requirement, the record's Branch value is also part of the access decision.

8. Administrator Requirement
The admin role is an exception to the normal role-based restrictions.

The ACL script contains:

if (gs.hasRole('admin')) {
    return true;
}

Therefore, administrators should be able to access records regardless of whether the Branch is EEE, ECE, or CSE.

The requirement can be represented as:

Admin
  |
  +---- EEE record → Allow
  |
  +---- ECE record → Allow
  |
  +---- CSE record → Allow

This requirement must be verified separately from normal-user testing.

9. Read ACL Requirement
The read ACL is the most important ACL in this lab because it demonstrates record-level access control.

The intended logic is:

                    User requests record
                            |
                            v
                     Is user admin?
                       /        \
                     Yes         No
                     |            |
                   ALLOW      Has bb1?
                                /   \
                              Yes    No
                               |      |
                            Check      DENY
                            Branch
                               |
                         Branch = EEE?
                           /       \
                         Yes       No
                          |         |
                        ALLOW      DENY

This provides the expected security behavior:

User	Branch	Expected Read
Admin	EEE	Allow
Admin	ECE	Allow
Admin	CSE	Allow
bb1 user	EEE	Allow
bb1 user	ECE	Deny
bb1 user	CSE	Deny
No bb1	EEE	Deny
No bb1	ECE	Deny
No bb1	CSE	Deny

Important requirement clarification: the ACL configuration should ensure that the Branch condition actually participates in the access decision. Simply checking gs.hasRole('bb1') in the script grants bb1 users access without checking whether the current record is EEE. If the requirement is truly "bb1 users can see only EEE records", the ACL logic must evaluate the current record's Branch value as well.

10. Create ACL Requirement
The create ACL must protect the creation operation independently from read access.

Requirement:

User has bb2 → Create allowed
User does not have bb2 → Create denied

For example:

User Roles	Create
bb1	Deny
bb1 + bb2	Allow
bb1 + bb2 + bb3	Allow
bb1 + bb2 + bb3 + bb4	Allow
admin	Allow

This demonstrates that having read permission does not automatically grant create permission.

11. Write ACL Requirement
The write ACL controls modification of existing records.

Requirement:

User has bb3 → Write allowed
User does not have bb3 → Write denied

The intended test user therefore needs:

bb1 + bb2 + bb3

to achieve the complete read/create/write workflow described in the lab.

12. Delete ACL Requirement
The delete ACL provides the highest level of destructive access in this lab.

Requirement:

User has bb4 → Delete allowed
User does not have bb4 → Delete denied

The complete test configuration is:

bb1 → Read
bb2 → Create
bb3 → Write
bb4 → Delete

This allows each ACL to be tested independently.

13. Non-Functional Security Requirements
The solution should also satisfy these security characteristics:

Least privilege
Users should not automatically receive create, write, or delete access merely because they can read records.

Separation of permissions
Each CRUD operation should have its own ACL requirement.

Record-level protection
Access must be determined at the record level where the Branch condition is applicable.

Administrator override
Administrators must retain full access for administration and troubleshooting.

Consistent enforcement
The ACLs should protect the table regardless of whether records are accessed through lists, forms, or other ServiceNow interfaces where the ACL applies.

Auditability
The configuration should be understandable and testable so that an administrator can determine why a particular user was allowed or denied access.

14. Assumptions
The requirement analysis assumes:

The tester has administrator access to the ServiceNow instance.

The security_admin role can be elevated when ACL configuration requires it.

The custom roles bb1–bb4 can be created.

The Branch field contains controlled choice values.

Test records exist for ECE, EEE, and CSE.

The test user can be impersonated for validation.

ACL changes are saved and available before testing.

Administrator access is intentionally unrestricted.

